    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Egyptian, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>, is with Sumerian one of the two earliest written languages, and its records span more than four thousand years, from the first hieroglyphs to Coptic.</li><li>It was written in hieroglyphs, a script of pictures standing for sounds and ideas, and in the faster hieratic and demotic, until the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> adopted the Coptic alphabet, based on Greek.</li><li>Forgotten after the last hieroglyphs were carved in 394, the script was deciphered by Jean-François Champollion in 1822 with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, reopening the history and literature of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>English words such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, oasis and gum come from ancient Egyptian through Greek, and adobe, the sun-dried brick, travelled from Egyptian through Coptic, Arabic and Spanish.</p></aside>
    </section>

